@node Privacy
@chapter Privacy
@cindex privacy

Codebud is privacy-first: by default nothing you type, nothing in your
project and nothing it runs leaves your machine, the data it keeps is
readable only by you, and every way data @emph{can} leave is a choice you
make, shown when it is in effect.  This chapter says exactly what that
covers and what it does not.

@menu
* Privacy at a Glance::          The defaults in one table.
* What Codebud Stores::          Files on disk, who can read them, masking.
* Encryption at Rest::           An age key sealed with your passphrase.
* Where Data Can Go::            Every outbound connection and its switch.
* Choosing How Private::         Privacy levels, isolation, unattended work.
* Checking Privacy::             doctor, config and the local-only test.
* What Privacy Does Not Cover::  The limits, plainly.
@end menu


@node Privacy at a Glance
@section Privacy at a Glance
@cindex privacy defaults

@multitable @columnfractions .34 .66
@headitem Topic @tab Default
@item Prompts and replies
@tab Sent only to the Ollama on this machine (@code{localhost:11434}).
@item Cloud models
@tab Never used unless you choose @env{CODEBUD_BACKEND=openai}; never a
fallback when Ollama is down.
@item Telemetry, update checks
@tab None.  Codebud contacts nothing on its own behalf.
@item Web search
@tab Off; when on, every query is shown and (outside @code{full-auto})
confirmed.
@item Command steps
@tab Isolated: no network, project-only writes, your secrets hidden.
@item Stored data
@tab Owner-only files (0700/0600); secrets masked as @samp{[redacted]}.
@item Retention
@tab Kept until you delete it; deletion lists what it removes and asks.
@item A cloned repository
@tab Cannot change any of the above through its @file{.codebud/config.toml}.
@end multitable


@node What Codebud Stores
@section What Codebud Stores
@cindex stored data

Everything lives in two directories (@pxref{Files and Directories}):

@table @file
@item ~/.local/share/codebud/
(@env{XDG_DATA_HOME}) --- @file{memory/entries.jsonl}, the history of
executed steps with their output; @file{sessions/}, saved chat
conversations; @file{index/}, project indexes with copies of your
files' text (@pxref{The index Subcommand}).
@item ~/.config/codebud/
(@env{XDG_CONFIG_HOME}) --- your @file{config.toml} and policy files.
@end table

@noindent
Nothing else is written outside your project, except what the steps you
approve do there.

@subheading Readable only by you

Directories are created 0700 and files 0600, whatever your
@code{umask}; @command{codebud doctor} warns if anything in these
directories is readable by other users and gives the @command{chmod}
that fixes it.

@subheading Secrets are masked

Before a session or a step is saved, API keys and tokens, private keys,
passwords in URLs and secret-looking assignments are replaced by
@samp{[redacted]} (@env{CODEBUD_MASK_SECRETS}, on by default).  What is
sent to the model is not changed.  Secret files (@file{.env},
@file{*.pem}, @file{id_rsa}, @dots{}) are never put into a project index.

@subheading Deleting

@example
codebud sessions --delete @var{name}         @r{# one conversation}
codebud sessions --purge [--older-than 30]  @r{# many}
codebud history --purge                     @r{# the step history}
codebud index --drop                        @r{# a project index}
@end example

@noindent
Each lists the files it removes and asks first (@option{--yes} for
scripts).  @code{session_retention_days} in your @file{config.toml}
deletes old sessions automatically (@pxref{The sessions Subcommand}).


@node Encryption at Rest
@section Encryption at Rest
@cindex encryption at rest
@cindex age encryption
@cindex passphrase
@cindex key subcommand

Codebud can encrypt the data it keeps with @uref{https://age-encryption.org,
age}.  It needs the optional @code{pyrage} module (@pxref{Python
Environment}); @command{codebud key status} says whether it is installed.

@subheading One key, sealed with your passphrase

@example
codebud key init        @r{# create the key; asks for a passphrase twice}
codebud key status      @r{# where the key is, and its public half}
codebud key passwd      @r{# change the passphrase}
@end example

@command{key init} creates one age key pair in
@file{~/.config/codebud/keys/} (@env{XDG_CONFIG_HOME}) --- never in the
data directory it protects:

@table @file
@item identity.age
The secret key, itself encrypted with your passphrase.  It is never
written to disk in the clear.
@item recipient.txt
The public key (@samp{age1@dots{}}).
@end table

@noindent
The directory is 0700 and both files 0600.  @command{key init} refuses to
replace an existing key, since data encrypted to it would become
unreadable.  @command{key passwd} re-seals the same key with a new
passphrase, so everything already encrypted stays readable.

@subheading When the passphrase is needed

Writing encrypted data needs only the public key: Codebud never asks for
the passphrase to save something.  Reading needs the secret key, so the
first read in a run asks for the passphrase on the terminal (three tries)
and keeps the unlocked key in memory until Codebud exits --- one prompt
per run, not one per file.  Unlocking takes about a second: age derives
the key from the passphrase with scrypt, deliberately slowly, to make
guessing expensive.

For unattended runs, the passphrase may come from @env{CODEBUD_PASSPHRASE}
instead (one try, since nobody is there to retype it).  Anything that can
read Codebud's environment can then unlock your data, so
@command{codebud doctor} warns while it is set; prefer the prompt.

@subheading If you lose the passphrase

The data encrypted to the key cannot be recovered --- not by you, not by
Codebud.  There is no back door and no recovery key.  Keep the passphrase
in a password manager; a copy of @file{identity.age} is only as safe as
the passphrase that seals it.

@subheading How the files are encrypted

Whole files are ordinary age files and can be decrypted with the
@command{age} tool and the unsealed key.  Files that are only ever
appended to cannot be appended to as one age file, so each line is
encrypted on its own and stored as @samp{age:} followed by the age file
in base64 --- still one line per entry.


@node Where Data Can Go
@section Where Data Can Go
@cindex outbound connections
@cindex egress

These are all the connections Codebud makes.  @command{codebud doctor}
lists the ones currently enabled on its @samp{Outbound:} line.

@table @asis
@item The Ollama server (@env{OLLAMA_BASE_URL})
Prompts --- your request, the conversation, retrieved project context ---
and text to embed for project search.  By default this is
@code{localhost}, so nothing leaves the machine.  A server on another
machine is announced when a command starts and shown in the chat
toolbar; over plain @code{http} it is refused unless you allow it, since
your code would cross the network unencrypted (@pxref{LLM Backend
Variables}).

@item OpenAI
Only with @env{CODEBUD_BACKEND=openai}: then prompts go to OpenAI, which
Codebud announces when a command starts and marks with @samp{☁ OpenAI}
in the chat toolbar.

@item DuckDuckGo
Only with @env{WEB_SEARCH_ENABLED=1}: the model's search queries go to
@code{lite.duckduckgo.com}.  Each query is printed, and confirmed before
it is sent except in @code{full-auto} mode (@pxref{web_search}).

@item Steps that declare @code{"network": true}
A command such as @command{pip install} may reach the internet when the
plan says it needs to; the step is shown and confirmed like a web search
(@pxref{Isolated Steps}).  Every other command step has no network.
@end table

@noindent
Outside Codebud: @command{ollama pull} downloads models from Ollama's
registry, and Ollama itself is configured separately; neither sends your
prompts anywhere.

A project's @file{.codebud/config.toml} cannot set any of these switches
(the backend, the Ollama URL, plain-http opt-in, the OpenAI key, web
search), nor the privacy level, secret masking or retention: cloning a
repository must never redirect your data or weaken your privacy.


@node Choosing How Private
@section Choosing How Private
@cindex privacy levels, overview

How much Codebud asks (the mode, @pxref{Agent Modes}) and what it may
reach (the privacy level) are separate dials:

@table @code
@item locked
Nothing leaves this machine: a backend that is not local, web search and
network steps are refused, and command steps must be isolated or they do
not run.
@item private
The default: everything above.
@item trusted
For trusted environments: command steps run unisolated, and web searches
and network steps run without asking.
@end table

@noindent
Choose with @option{--privacy}, @code{/privacy} in @command{chat}, or
@code{privacy =} in your @file{config.toml} (@pxref{Privacy Levels}).
@strong{No level lifts the safety rules}: the allow-list, the refused
arguments and the confirmation of powerful commands always apply.

Fully unattended work is always possible: @code{full-auto} mode asks
nothing, at the default level too; @code{trusted} additionally drops
isolation.  The built-in profile @code{unattended} sets both
(@option{--profile unattended}, @pxref{Profiles}).  The level in effect is announced, shown in the toolbar and
recorded in the session file; a resumed session never silently becomes
@code{trusted}.


@node Checking Privacy
@section Checking Privacy
@cindex privacy, checking

@example
codebud doctor
@end example

@noindent
reports, among the rest:

@example
  [PASS] LLM backend: ollama at http://localhost:11434 (local)
  [PASS] Privacy: private (data leaves only where you chose)
  [PASS] Isolation: bubblewrap: no network, project-only writes, secrets hidden
  [PASS] Outbound: none - prompts, code and searches stay on this machine
  [PASS] Data and config files are private (owner only)
@end example

@command{codebud config} shows each setting and where it came from
(environment, project or user @file{config.toml}, default).  The test
suite includes @file{tests/test_local_only.py}, which refuses every
connection to an address that is not this machine and runs planning,
execution, indexing and search against a local Ollama --- with an OpenAI
key present --- so a change that reached outward would fail it.


@node What Privacy Does Not Cover
@section What Privacy Does Not Cover
@cindex privacy, limits

@itemize
@item
@strong{What you send is what the backend sees.}  With a remote Ollama or
the OpenAI backend, your requests, conversation and retrieved project
context go there; their privacy is that server's.

@item
@strong{File tools are not isolated.}  @code{file_read},
@code{file_write} and @code{patch} run inside Codebud, with your rights;
only command steps are sandboxed (@pxref{What Is Not Protected}).

@item
@strong{Isolation needs bubblewrap and user namespaces.}  On macOS, the
BSDs, in many containers and where user namespaces are blocked, command
steps are not isolated (a warning at @code{private}, refused at
@code{locked}).

@item
@strong{Masking is pattern-based.}  It catches common token formats and
secret-looking assignments, not every secret; the project index keeps
your files' text as it is.

@item
@strong{Your terminal and Ollama are your own.}  Scrollback, shell
history and Ollama's logs are outside Codebud's control.
@end itemize
